<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d6" for="edge" attr.name="source_id" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="edge" attr.name="keywords" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="edge" attr.name="description" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d3" for="edge" attr.name="weight" attr.type="double" />
  <key id="d2" for="node" attr.name="source_id" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="node" attr.name="description" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="node" attr.name="entity_type" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="&quot;CATHERINE OF HENNEBERG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Catherine of Henneberg was a member of the noble House of Henneberg, born in 1334, and married to William I, Margrave of Meissen, later becoming the Landgrave of Thuringia. She formed a union with Zanaida Fernández-Maquieira y Fernández-Maquieira through a temporal anomaly."&lt;SEP&gt;"Catherine of Henneberg was married to Frederick III and played a significant role in German noble history."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ZANAIDA FERNÁNDEZ-MAQUIEIRA Y FERNÁNDEZ-MAQUIEIRA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Zanaida Fernández-Maquieira y Fernández-Maquieira was born in 1928 in a small town in Spain and formed a union with Catherine of Henneberg through a temporal anomaly."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WILLIAM I, MARGRAVE OF MEISSEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"William I, Margrave of Meissen was Catherine of Henneberg's husband and later her spouse in Thuringia."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LANDGRAVE OF THURINGIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ROLE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Landgrave of Thuringia was a title held by Catherine of Henneberg after her marriage to Frederick III."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FREDERICK III, LANDGRAVE OF THURINGIA AND MARGRAVE OF MEISSEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Frederick III was the Margrave of Meissen and later the Landgrave of Thuringia, married to Catherine of Henneberg in the 14th century."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THURINGIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thuringia is a region where Catherine of Henneberg and Frederick III ruled and where the union with Zanaida Fernández-Maquieira y Fernández-Maquieira is said to have occurred through a temporal anomaly."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEISSEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Meissen is a region where Catherine of Henneberg and Frederick III were Margraves and where the union with Zanaída Fernández-Maquieira y Fernández-Maquieira is said to have occurred through a temporal anomaly."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;JOURNAL OF TEMPORAL ANOMALIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Journal of Temporal Anomalies published a study suggesting a temporal anomaly allowed Catherine of Henneberg and Zanaida Fernández-Maquieira y Fernández-Maquieira to meet."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF CHRONO-SOCIOLOGY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"International Journal of Chrono-Sociology published a paper presenting evidence of a temporal anomaly in the 14th century."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INSTITUTE OF HISTORICAL RESEARCH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Institute of Historical Research conducted a genealogical analysis linking Catherine of Henneberg to Zanaida Fernández-Maquieira y Fernández-Maquieira through a complex network of familial relationships."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THURINGIAN CHRONICLE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DOCUMENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thuringian Chronicle is a historical document from the late 14th century that provides evidence of Catherine of Henneberg and Frederick III's marriage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thuringian Chronicle written by Johannes Rothe in the late 14th century explicitly states that Frederick III took Catherine of Henneberg as his wife in 1344."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEISSEN URKUNDENBUCH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DOCUMENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Meissen Urkundenbuch is a collection of documents that also provides evidence of Catherine of Henneberg and Frederick III's marriage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Meissen Urkundenbuch, a collection of medieval charters and documents, includes a charter dated 1356 referring to Catherine as Frederick III's 'uxor dilectissima'."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DR. SOPHIA PATEL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dr. Sophia Patel is a researcher who published a paper on temporal disruptions and inter-era relationships."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DR. MARIA H. OEHLER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dr. Maria H. Oehler is a historian specializing in medieval German nobility and confirmed the marriage between Catherine of Henneberg and Frederick III."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;JOHANNES ROTHE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Johannes Rothe is the author of the Thuringian Chronicle, which mentions the marriage between Frederick III and Catherine of Henneberg."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FREDERICK III&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Frederick III was married to Catherine of Henneberg and ruled together with her."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GERMAN NOBLE HISTORY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"German noble history includes the marriage of Catherine of Henneberg and Frederick III, which is documented in medieval records."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEDIEVAL RECORDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Medieval records provide evidence of Catherine of Henneberg and Frederick III's marriage, contributing to their place in history."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXPERT TESTIMONY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Expert testimony supports the evidence from medieval records, confirming the marriage of Catherine of Henneberg and Frederick III."</data>
      <data key="d2">cb-0323</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="&quot;CATHERINE OF HENNEBERG&quot;" target="&quot;ZANAIDA FERNÁNDEZ-MAQUIEIRA Y FERNÁNDEZ-MAQUIEIRA&quot;">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Catherine of Henneberg and Zanaida Fernández-Maquieira y Fernández-Maquieira formed a union through a temporal anomaly, transcending their respective eras."</data>
      <data key="d5">"temporal anomaly, inter-era relationship"</data>
      <data key="d6">cb-0323</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;CATHERINE OF HENNEBERG&quot;" target="&quot;WILLIAM I, MARGRAVE OF MEISSEN&quot;">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Catherine of Henneberg was initially married to William I, Margrave of Meissen, before becoming the Landgrave of Thuringia with Frederick III."</data>
      <data key="d5">"marriage, succession"</data>
      <data key="d6">cb-0323</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;CATHERINE OF HENNEBERG&quot;" target="&quot;FREDERICK III, LANDGRAVE OF THURINGIA AND MARGRAVE OF MEISSEN&quot;">
      <data key="d3">10.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Catherine of Henneberg and Frederick III were married and ruled together, strengthening the position of their respective families in medieval politics."</data>
      <data key="d5">"marriage, political alliance"</data>
      <data key="d6">cb-0323</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;CATHERINE OF HENNEBERG&quot;" target="&quot;FREDERICK III&quot;">
      <data key="d3">20.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Catherine of Henneberg was married to Frederick III and they ruled together."</data>
      <data key="d5">"marriage, joint rule"</data>
      <data key="d6">cb-0323</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;CATHERINE OF HENNEBERG&quot;" target="&quot;THURINGIAN CHRONICLE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The Thuringian Chronicle provides evidence of Catherine of Henneberg and Frederick III's marriage."</data>
      <data key="d5">"documentary evidence, historical record"</data>
      <data key="d6">cb-0323</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;CATHERINE OF HENNEBERG&quot;" target="&quot;MEISSEN URKUNDENBUCH&quot;">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The Meissen Urkundenbuch also provides evidence of Catherine of Henneberg and Frederick III's marriage."</data>
      <data key="d5">"documentary evidence, historical record"</data>
      <data key="d6">cb-0323</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;CATHERINE OF HENNEBERG&quot;" target="&quot;GERMAN NOBLE HISTORY&quot;">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Catherine of Henneberg's marriage to Frederick III is part of the annals of German noble history."</data>
      <data key="d5">"historical significance, documentation"</data>
      <data key="d6">cb-0323</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;CATHERINE OF HENNEBERG&quot;" target="&quot;MEDIEVAL RECORDS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
